rogressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbacbde3b-1b25-4629-a969-1e4cbbca4166_1000x1080.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!1k_f!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbacbde3b-1b25-4629-a969-1e4cbbca4166_1000x1080.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!1k_f!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbacbde3b-1b25-4629-a969-1e4cbbca4166_1000x1080.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/bacbde3b-1b25-4629-a969-1e4cbbca4166_1000x1080.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!1k_f!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbacbde3b-1b25-4629-a969-1e4cbbca4166_1000x1080.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!1k_f!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbacbde3b-1b25-4629-a969-1e4cbbca4166_1000x1080.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!1k_f!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbacbde3b-1b25-4629-a969-1e4cbbca4166_1000x1080.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!1k_f!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fbacbde3b-1b25-4629-a969-1e4cbbca4166_1000x1080.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>This technique has been used to make fluorescence imaging much easier in live animals. </p><p>On my end, doing this well could add to the set of things that independent scientists could image at home. We might be able to probe internal structures of things more casually. Making things transparent would give better images and would allow independent scientists to look at tissue structure more closely.</p><p>Effective observation at low cost is one of the themes of my research. I wrote about writing tracing software for Brownian particles using my microscope in my last post. I am also thinking about trying to match the performance of CO2 gas sensing using pressure, which would make that type of sensing cheaper. In line with this theme, I’m super excited about attempting weird and cool techniques to make structures more easier to probe!</p><p>Here’s is more than you ever wanted to know about optical transparency. </p><p>A material can be opaque for two reasons. First, a material can absorb light and block it. Second, it can scramble light due to different refractive indices.</p><p><span>First, light rays from the other side could be absorbed when they pass through. If light is absorbed, the light can't hit your eyes and you can't see the other side. Light can have multiple wavelengths, and the wavelengths absorbed by the material are determined by the material's </span><em>spectra</em><span>. To calculate the spectra, we can approximate the physical behaviour of molecules to that of an electron around a potential. The Schrödinger equation tells us that this system can only occupy a discrete set of energies, and it tells us which ones these are. The differences between these energies set the wavelengths that are ultimately absorbed by the material.</span></p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Ff9n!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5121ae3f-7ba7-44f2-8787-9be7f582c2d3_1296x881.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>In reality, materials are composed of lots of different things and so we expect them to also absorb different wavelengths.</p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!PDA4!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F9ed05ca7-3e5e-458d-87c2-c678925b756e_2427x1012.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>Another reason for opacity is less obvious, but more interesting. The refractive index of a material tells us how quickly light travels within the medium. When light travels between materials with different refractive indices, it deflects slightly. When a material is packed with sub-materials that have different refractive indices, the refraction within causes light rays to get scattered since light rays ping around.</p><p>Biological materials contain a mix of different sub-materials inside - fibres, cells, proteins, and more. Each of these materials contains a different refractive index. When you have a material with one refractive index embedded in a material with a different refractive index, you get scattering. This scattering causes the light from the other side to get scrambled. The diagram below shows this.</p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!96Ys!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1e15a795-695f-4cf4-9daf-2431aca08a68_2278x1026.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>We can model biological materials as a 'base material' of water, with chunks of lipids and proteins embedded inside, all of which have different refractive indices.</p><p>To make something transparent, we need to exploit the two causes of opacity. One approach is to try to change the absorbance spectra of a material so that it absorbs fewer wavelengths of light.</p><p>Alternatively (and this is the approach we'll cover in this post), we could try to change the refractive index of the water inside to match the embedded media. The image below shows this principle with biological tissues. If you can change the refractive index of water with a dye, you can then match it to the refractive index of proteins and lipids, which is slightly higher.</p><p>If you manage to do this without modifying the absorbance of a material, you should be able to make it more transparent.</p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/ecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!oT0Y!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fecb8be71-438d-49de-97f4-bb8cd51f41e2_2464x1050.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>Ok, so the game is to change the refractive index. Here is a map of the calculations we need to do to figure out how to modify the refractive index of a material.</p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!BTay!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5e3b636d-0da7-4c0b-bee4-7e91dee9bcbc_1968x1358.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>To understand where the refractive index plays a part in a quantitative model, let's model how light travels according to Maxwell. Light is an electromagnetic wave, comprised of oscillating electric and magnetic waves. For this essay, let's focus on the electric field part to get the key intuitions.</p><p>Using complex representation, if light travels down the z axis, in a vacuum, we can write down the equation of light as the following. Because it's a wave, we can write</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(E(z, t) = E_0 \cos\!\left(\frac{\omega z}{c} - \omega t\right)\)</span></p></div><p>But for convenience we use complex numbers</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(E(z, t) = \mathrm{Re}\left\{ E_0\, e^{\,i\omega z / c \;-\; i\omega t} \right\}\)</span></p></div><p><span>Where E₀ is the amplitude (how strong the field is), ω is the angular frequency (at a fixed point the field goes round once every T = 2π/ω), </span><em>c</em><span> is the speed of light, </span><em>z</em><span> is distance along the beam and </span><em>t</em><span> is time. Crest to crest, the wavelength is λ = 2πc/ω.</span></p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/ff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!sKv7!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_lossy/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fff4532be-0334-4133-803a-8f1d4b059f99_1100x520.gif 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>In media though, we expect both the speed of light to change in the medium, as well as some absorption. We can rewrite the equation as follows</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(E(z, t) = \mathrm{Re}\left\{ E_0\, e^{\,i(n' + i\kappa)\,\omega z / c \;-\; i\omega t} \right\} = \mathrm{Re}\left\{ E_0\, e^{-\kappa \omega z / c}\, e^{\,i(n' \omega z / c \;-\; \omega t)} \right\}\)</span></p></div><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/e6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mZST!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fe6f49656-7fdc-4d0f-9ba4-052a9099fe17_1862x778.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>The coefficient in the exponential n′ is a multiplier behind the spatial term, and represents the real refractive index. κ represents how much the wave attenuates, and is meant to denote absorption.</p><p>In practice we can group n′ + iκ as one number, and then refer to this as the complex refractive index.</p><p>First we need to try to change the real refractive index. If we can increase the refractive index of water to match the tissue refractive index, we would have a more transparent material. Water has a refractive index of 1.33. And although this number is variable, protein and lipid rich structures have a refractive index of around 1.45. So the game is to adjust the refractive index of the water around it towards 1.45, which would make it more transparent.</p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/b856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!Xo-M!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fb856226f-c10b-45e3-a91e-0e367106a600_1832x883.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>Whatever we do to the water, we also need to make sure that we don't make the absorbance property too high. If κ gets too high, then we'd end up absorbing all the light and it won't go transparent.</p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!aawS!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F07948d7a-a26a-40b6-bb49-34955a968f48_1830x1331.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>So how do we shift the refractive index? To understand refractive indices, we first need to understand polarisation. To first order, a material consists of electrons bound to nuclei. When an electric field is applied to a material, the relative positions of the electrons to their nuclei change.</p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!VHPy!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F138baf18-54de-438a-8839-d90ce554c2cc_354x270.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p><a href="http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/electric/dielec.html#c1">from [http://hyperphysics.phy-astr.gsu.edu/hbase/electric/dielec.html#c1</a><span>]</span></p><p>For a positively charged particle and a negatively charged particle, polarisation is the magnitude of that charge multiplied by the displacement of one from the other. To calculate the polarisation, we need to model how far electrons displace from the nucleus under the electric field.</p><p>Electric fields provide a force on charges that are free. To model the effect of an electric field on a material, we treat the positive nucleus as fixed, and the electrons as free. To calculate the effect of the system, we'll use the Lorentz oscillator model.</p><p>In this model, the electrons are modelled to be bound to the nucleus like a damped spring, with the electric field applying a forcing. We can solve it exactly how we would solve a damped spring problem:</p><div><figure><a target="_blank" href="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png" data-component-name="Image2ToDOM"><div><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png 1456w" sizes="100vw"><img src="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png" width="728" height="409.5" data-attrs="{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/c066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:false,&quot;imageSize&quot;:&quot;normal&quot;,&quot;height&quot;:819,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:728,&quot;bytes&quot;:null,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;captionedImage&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}" alt="" srcset="https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!KWEd!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc066d1b9-a906-4160-b712-04806ff5b917_1917x900.png 1456w" sizes="100vw" loading="lazy"></picture></div></a></figure></div><p>Newton's second law for the electron, with the light's field oscillating at ω:</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(m\ddot{x} = -m\omega_0^2\, x \;-\; m\gamma\,\dot{x} \;-\; e\,E_0\, e^{-i\omega t}\)</span></p></div><p>If we try an Ansatz for the position of the electron x(t) = x₀ e^(−iωt) (the electron oscillates at the driving frequency), we get that the position of the electron relative to the nucleus is given by the following expression.</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(x(t) = \frac{-e/m}{\omega_0^2 - \omega^2 - i\gamma\omega}\; E(t)\)</span></p></div><p><span>The dipole moment is p = −ex, and with </span><em>N</em><span> electrons per unit volume the polarisation per unit volume is</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\mathbf{P} = N p = \frac{N e^2/m}{\omega_0^2 - \omega^2 - i\gamma\omega}\; \mathbf{E}\)</span></p></div><p>In this setup, ω₀ represents the natural frequency of the bound electron, which is the frequency the molecule absorbs at (for tartrazine, the 425 nm peak), γ represents the damping rate, which sets how broad that absorption peak is, and ω is the frequency of the electric field. Now we've found polarisation, we want to relate it to the refractive index itself. To do that, we need to do some gymnastics with two identities.</p><p>By convention, the polarisation can be written as proportional to the electric field</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\mathbf{P} = \varepsilon_0\,\chi\,\mathbf{E}\)</span></p></div><p><span>where ε₀ is the permittivity of free space, χ is the electric susceptibility (a dimensionless number that says how easily the material polarises), and </span><strong>E</strong><span> is the electric field. Comparing with the Lorentz oscillator result above,</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\chi(\omega) = \frac{N e^2/(\varepsilon_0 m)}{\omega_0^2 - \omega^2 - i\gamma\omega}\)</span></p></div><p><span>To relate our polarisation, and electric susceptibility to the refractive index, we use the following trick. </span><strong>The below is an AI-assisted proof with manual checking.</strong></p><p><span>This degree of polarisation and the electric field is encoded in something called the </span><strong>electric displacement field D</strong><span>. And the relationship between D and the electric field is linear, with the coefficient called the relative permittivity, εᵣ. </span><strong>P</strong><span> here represents the polarisation per unit volume, and is the average induced dipole moment across all of the material.</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\mathbf{D} = \varepsilon_0 \varepsilon_r \mathbf{E} = \varepsilon_0 \mathbf{E} + \mathbf{P}\)</span></p></div><p>It turns out that the relative permittivity is related to the complex refractive index as follows:</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(n_c^2 = (n' + i\kappa)^2 = \varepsilon_r\)</span></p></div><p>The proof of the relationship between refractive index and permittivity for a standard electric wave is standard, and is in the footnote below.² And so, to change the complex refractive index, we need to change the relative permittivity of the material.</p><p>To proceed, we use an approximation. The electric displacement field is a function of the polarisation of a material. But when we mix two materials together, we can add their polarisation densities.</p><p> Before adding tartrazine, we have that water obeys</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\mathbf{D} = \varepsilon_0\,\varepsilon_{r,\mathrm{water}}\,\mathbf{E} = \varepsilon_0\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{water}}\)</span></p></div><p>Then when we add the dye, we get</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\mathbf{D} = \varepsilon_0\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{water}} + \mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{tartrazine}}\)</span></p></div><p><span>But we know that each polarisation is proportional to the field, with its own susceptibility: </span><strong>P</strong><span>(water) = ε₀ χ(water) </span><strong>E</strong><span> and </span><strong>P</strong><span>(tartrazine) = ε₀ χ(dye) </span><strong>E</strong><span>, where χ(dye). Substituting that in, we get </span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\mathbf{D} = \varepsilon_0\left(1 + \chi_w + \chi_d\right)\mathbf{E}\)</span></p></div><p><span>Comparing with </span><strong>D</strong><span> = ε₀εᵣ</span><strong>E</strong><span>, and using that water alone has εᵣ(water) = 1 + χ(water) = n(water)², the relative permittivity of the dyed water is</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\varepsilon_r = \varepsilon_{r,\mathrm{water}} + \chi_d = n_w^2 + \chi_d\)</span></p></div><p>For a dilute dye, χ(dye) is small compared to n(water)², so we can expand the square root and use a Taylor expansion to get the modified refractive index of the water with the dye. </p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(n_c = \sqrt{n_w^2 + \chi_d} = n_w\sqrt{1 + \frac{\chi_d}{n_w^2}} \;\approx\; n_w + \frac{\chi_d}{2 n_w}\)</span></p></div><p>n_c is complex. If we write it out as n_c = n′ + iκ and split the electric susceptibility of  χ(dye) that we got into real and imaginary parts, the dye shifts the real refractive index and introduces absorption by</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\Delta n' = n' - n_w = \frac{\mathrm{Re}\,\chi_d}{2 n_w}, \qquad \kappa = \frac{\mathrm{Im}\,\chi_d}{2 n_w}\)</span></p></div><p>And putting in the Lorentz form of χ(dye) that we got earlier</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\Delta n' = \frac{N e^2}{2 n_w \varepsilon_0 m}\,\frac{\omega_0^2 - \omega^2}{(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)^2 + \gamma^2\omega^2}, \qquad \kappa = \frac{N e^2}{2 n_w \varepsilon_0 m}\,\frac{\gamma\omega}{(\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)^2 + \gamma^2\omega^2}\)</span></p></div><p>For tartrazine and red light, ω is below the resonance ω₀, so ω₀² − ω² &gt; 0 and Δn′ is positive. This means the dye raises the refractive index of the water. Meanwhile κ carries a factor of γω in the numerator and is only large near ω ≈ ω₀,, which means that we don't get absorbance.</p><p><strong>References</strong></p><p>Ou Z, Duh YS, Rommelfanger NJ, Keck CHC, Jiang S, Brinson K Jr, Zhao S, Schmidt EL, Wu X, Yang F, Cai B, Cui H, Qi W, Wu S, Tantry A, Roth R, Ding J, Chen X, Kaltschmidt JA, Brongersma ML, Hong G. Achieving optical transparency in live animals with absorbing molecules. Science. 2024 Sep 6;385(6713):eadm6869. doi: 10.1126/science.adm6869. Epub 2024 Sep 6. PMID: 39236186; PMCID: PMC11931656.</p><p><strong>²</strong><span>  Here are Maxwell's equations for electric and magnetic fields in a material. This is generated by Claude.</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\nabla \cdot \mathbf{D} = \rho_f, \qquad \nabla \cdot \mathbf{B} = 0, \qquad \nabla \times \mathbf{E} = -\frac{\partial \mathbf{B}}{\partial t}, \qquad \nabla \times \mathbf{H} = \mathbf{J}_f + \frac{\partial \mathbf{D}}{\partial t}\)</span></p></div><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\mathbf{D} = \varepsilon_0 \mathbf{E} + \mathbf{P} = \varepsilon_0 \varepsilon_r \mathbf{E}, \qquad \mathbf{B} = \mu_0 \mathbf{H} \quad \text{(non-magnetic)}\)</span></p></div><p>The question is then - how do any of these relate to the refractive index?</p><p><span>No free charges or currents (ρ = 0, </span><strong>J</strong><span> = 0), non-magnetic. Faraday's law says that the curl of an electric field is the negative of the magnetic field's time derivative. Take the curl of Faraday's law, and then substitute in</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\nabla \times (\nabla \times \mathbf{E}) = -\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\,\nabla \times \mathbf{B}\)</span></p></div><p><span>Ampère–Maxwell with </span><strong>J</strong><span> = 0:</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\nabla \times \mathbf{H} = \frac{\partial \mathbf{D}}{\partial t}\)</span></p></div><p><span>Substitute </span><strong>B</strong><span> = μ₀</span><strong>H</strong><span> and </span><strong>D</strong><span> = ε₀</span><strong>E</strong><span> + </span><strong>P</strong><span> = ε₀εᵣ</span><strong>E</strong><span>:</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\nabla \times \mathbf{B} = \mu_0 \frac{\partial \mathbf{D}}{\partial t} = \mu_0 \varepsilon_0 \frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial t} + \mu_0 \frac{\partial \mathbf{P}}{\partial t} = \mu_0 \varepsilon_0 \varepsilon_r \frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial t}\)</span></p></div><p><span>This is where εᵣ enters: the polarisation current ∂</span><strong>P</strong><span>/∂t adds to the vacuum displacement current. Then (taking εᵣ constant in time, i.e. working at a single frequency)</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\nabla \times (\nabla \times \mathbf{E}) = -\frac{\partial}{\partial t}\,\nabla \times \mathbf{B} = -\mu_0 \varepsilon_0 \varepsilon_r \frac{\partial^2 \mathbf{E}}{\partial t^2}\)</span></p></div><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\nabla \times (\nabla \times \mathbf{E}) = \nabla(\nabla \cdot \mathbf{E}) - \nabla^2 \mathbf{E} = -\nabla^2 \mathbf{E}\)</span></p></div><p>Then if we equate both sides, we get a plane wave equation</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\Rightarrow \quad \nabla^2 \mathbf{E} = \frac{\varepsilon_r}{c^2}\frac{\partial^2 \mathbf{E}}{\partial t^2}, \qquad c^2 = \frac{1}{\mu_0 \varepsilon_0}\)</span></p></div><p><span>Plane wave </span><strong>E</strong><span> = </span><strong>E</strong><span>₀ e^(i(kz − ωt))</span></p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(k^2 = \varepsilon_r \frac{\omega^2}{c^2}\)</span></p></div><p>Comparing with k = (n′ + iκ) ω/c from the attenuating wave written earlier,</p><div data-component-name="Latex"><p><span>\(\varepsilon_r(\omega) = n_c(\omega)^2 = (n' + i\kappa)^2 = n'^2 - \kappa^2 + 2 i n' \kappa\)</span></p></div></div></article></div><div><div id="discussion"><h4>Discussion about this post</h4></div><div><h3>Ready for more?</h3></div></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
