            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/home-assistant/home-assistant.io/issues/49314">https://github.com/home-assistant/home-assistant.io/issues/49314</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50124251">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><div data-testid="issue-viewer-issue-container" data-hpc="true" containertiming="hpc" tabindex="-1"><h2>Description</h2><div><div><p><a data-component="Link" href="https://github.com/Viss" aria-label="@Viss's profile"><img data-component="Avatar" alt="@Viss" width="24" height="24" src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1756255?u=7ed4ec158e6547add67647f555631a64471c865a&amp;v=4&amp;size=48" data-testid="github-avatar"></a></p></div><div id="issue-body-viewer" data-testid="issue-body-viewer"><div data-testid="markdown-body" data-team-hovercards-enabled="true" data-turbolinks="false"><h3 dir="auto">Type of issue</h3>
<p dir="auto">Other (issue in content formatting, broken link or image, non-working widget)</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Problem description</h3>
<p dir="auto">This is a pretty severe invasion of privacy.<br>
I never authorized any integrations or plugins for scanning secrets and sending them up the line, even if they are hashed or not.<br>
This mechanism could be abused by a malicious threat actor with access to the home assistant github repos in a variety of ways.</p>
<p dir="auto">how do i turn this thing off?</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Suggested solution</h3>
<p dir="auto">make this an opt-in feature, do not just opt people in to scanning for secrets with a system they were not aware existed.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">URL</h3>
<p dir="auto"><a href="https://www.home-assistant.io/more-info/pwned-passwords/" rel="nofollow">https://www.home-assistant.io/more-info/pwned-passwords/</a></p>
<h3 dir="auto">Version</h3>
<p dir="auto">2026.10.1</p>
<h3 dir="auto">How do you know the suggested solution is correct?</h3>
<p dir="auto"><em>No response</em></p>
<h3 dir="auto">Additional information</h3>
<p dir="auto"><em>No response</em></p></div><!--$--><div data-testid="pinned-comment-display"><div><p dir="auto">Leaving this closed since this repository is for our documentation, but I want to follow up, because my earlier replies were more dismissive than this deserved. My apologies.</p>
<p dir="auto">As it turns out, this check can already be turned off after all:</p>
<div data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="ha security options --pwned=false"><pre><code>ha security options --pwned=false
</code></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">To be transparent: this check isn't new, it has been part of the Supervisor for years. It checks app options that are marked as passwords. A recent release fixed the warning that surfaces it, which is why you're seeing it now.</p>
<p dir="auto">Privacy was a design requirement for this feature from the start, which is why your secrets never leave your system. The Supervisor hashes them locally and sends only the first 5 characters of th…</p></div><p><a data-component="Link" href="#issuecomment-6224767556">View full comment </a></p></div><!--/$--></div></div></div><!--$--><div data-testid="issue-viewer-comments-container"><h2 id="_R_bnnd685_">Activity</h2><ol id="issue-timeline" data-testid="issue-timeline-container" aria-labelledby="_R_bnnd685_" role="list"><li data-timeline-event-id="LE_lADOAbAFe88AAAABXwwfDM8AAAAHyzzBfA" data-highlighted-event="false" data-testid="timeline-row-border-LE_lADOAbAFe88AAAABXwwfDM8AAAAHyzzBfA" data-wrapper-timeline-id="LE_lADOAbAFe88AAAABXwwfDM8AAAAHyzzBfA"></li><li data-wrapper-timeline-id="IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcunnnA"><div data-testid="timeline-row-border-IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcunnnA" data-timeline-event-id="IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcunnnA" data-highlighted-event="false"><div id="issuecomment-6222899100" data-testid="comment-header"><h3>frenck<!-- --> <!-- -->commented<!-- --> <relative-time data-component="RelativeTime">on<!-- --> <!-- -->Oct 16, 2026<!-- --> </relative-time></h3><p><a data-component="Link" href="https://github.com/frenck" aria-label="@frenck's profile"><img data-component="Avatar" alt="@frenck" width="24" height="24" src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/195327?u=c0f88e376380f7a811559aa54d5096a66f6d3488&amp;v=4&amp;size=48" data-testid="github-avatar"></a></p></div><div data-testid="markdown-body" data-team-hovercards-enabled="true" data-turbolinks="false"><p dir="auto">Oh hi there 👋</p>
<p dir="auto">It is not an invasion of privacy and there are sure other apps out there doing this (most notably the 1Password password manager). Your passwords are never send out, never published.</p>
<p dir="auto">Read more about it here: <a href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/validating-leaked-passwords-with-k-anonymity/" rel="nofollow">https://blog.cloudflare.com/validating-leaked-passwords-with-k-anonymity/</a></p>
<p dir="auto">As a matter of fact, it is designed to handled these types of data with privacy in mind, see also: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K-anonymity" rel="nofollow">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K-anonymity</a></p>
<p dir="auto">If anything, learn from the output, because the secrets you are using are listed in the password databases and thus easily "hackable".</p>
<p dir="auto">../Frenck</p>
