</p>
<p dir="auto">Blogging my personal ramblings at <a href="https://frenck.dev/" rel="nofollow">frenck.dev</a></p></div></div></li><li data-wrapper-timeline-id="IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcvipmQ"><div data-testid="timeline-row-border-IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcvipmQ" data-timeline-event-id="IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcvipmQ" data-highlighted-event="false"><div id="issuecomment-6223866265" data-testid="comment-header"><h3>troed<!-- --> <!-- -->commented<!-- --> <relative-time data-component="RelativeTime">on<!-- --> <!-- -->Oct 16, 2026<!-- --> </relative-time></h3><p><a data-component="Link" href="https://github.com/troed" aria-label="@troed's profile"><img data-component="Avatar" alt="@troed" width="24" height="24" src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/931070?u=d69a22b45848dcb0d58207631d646b3c19f98cb5&amp;v=4&amp;size=48" data-testid="github-avatar"></a></p></div><div data-testid="markdown-body" data-team-hovercards-enabled="true" data-turbolinks="false"><blockquote>
<p dir="auto">It is not an invasion of privacy</p>
</blockquote>
<p dir="auto">Yes. Yes it is.</p>
<p dir="auto">/whitehat hacker</p></div></div></li><li data-timeline-event-id="LOE_lADOAbAFe88AAAABXwwfDM8AAAAHy5EnVg" data-highlighted-event="false" data-testid="timeline-row-border-LOE_lADOAbAFe88AAAABXwwfDM8AAAAHy5EnVg" data-wrapper-timeline-id="LOE_lADOAbAFe88AAAABXwwfDM8AAAAHy5EnVg"><div><p>locked </p><!-- --><p>as too heated </p><!-- --><p>and limited conversation to collaborators </p><a data-component="Link" href="https://github.com/home-assistant/home-assistant.io/issues/49314#event-33480058710"><relative-time data-component="RelativeTime">on Oct 16, 2026</relative-time></a></div></li><li data-wrapper-timeline-id="IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcwZqRA"><div data-testid="timeline-row-border-IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcwZqRA" data-timeline-event-id="IC_kwDOAbAFe88AAAABcwZqRA" data-highlighted-event="false"><div id="issuecomment-6224767556" data-testid="comment-header"><h3>frenck<!-- --> <!-- -->commented<!-- --> <relative-time data-component="RelativeTime">on<!-- --> <!-- -->Oct 16, 2026<!-- --> </relative-time></h3><p><a data-component="Link" href="https://github.com/frenck" aria-label="@frenck's profile"><img data-component="Avatar" alt="@frenck" width="24" height="24" src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/195327?u=c0f88e376380f7a811559aa54d5096a66f6d3488&amp;v=4&amp;size=48" data-testid="github-avatar"></a></p></div><div data-testid="markdown-body" data-team-hovercards-enabled="true" data-turbolinks="false"><p dir="auto">Leaving this closed since this repository is for our documentation, but I want to follow up, because my earlier replies were more dismissive than this deserved. My apologies.</p>
<p dir="auto">As it turns out, this check can already be turned off after all:</p>
<div data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="ha security options --pwned=false"><pre><code>ha security options --pwned=false
</code></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">To be transparent: this check isn't new, it has been part of the Supervisor for years. It checks app options that are marked as passwords. A recent release fixed the warning that surfaces it, which is why you're seeing it now.</p>
<p dir="auto">Privacy was a design requirement for this feature from the start, which is why your secrets never leave your system. The Supervisor hashes them locally and sends only the first 5 characters of the SHA-1 hash to Have I Been Pwned. HIBP returns every hash starting with that prefix, and the matching happens on your own instance. You can see exactly what it does here: <a href="https://github.com/home-assistant/supervisor/blob/main/supervisor/utils/pwned.py">https://github.com/home-assistant/supervisor/blob/main/supervisor/utils/pwned.py</a></p>
<p dir="auto">You are right that our documentation should have explained this, including how to turn it off. We'll get that page updated.</p>
