            <title><![CDATA[You could have invented transformers from memory (419 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://alexwang.ai/posts/associative-memory/</link>
            <guid>50122878</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:01:17 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://alexwang.ai/posts/associative-memory/">https://alexwang.ai/posts/associative-memory/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50122878">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><figure><svg viewBox="0 0 564 142" role="img" aria-label="Four pictures of the character 記, meaning to remember, each recalled from a different memory store: an outer product, an MLP with a ReLU, a gated MLP, and self-attention. As the number of stored memories grows from 16 to 2048, the outer product's picture dissolves into static first, then the ReLU MLP's, then the gated MLP's turns grainy, while self-attention's stays sharp. Each picture is red while its recall works and turns to ink once interference outweighs the signal."></svg><figcaption>Retrieving a character from memory. The recall worsens as the number of memories grows.</figcaption></figure><p>Large language models today can recall obscure facts from every corner of the world.
Most recently, I was running late for a friend’s board game night, so I asked Astra to remind me the rules to Dune: Imperium.
It gladly narrated them to me as I drove over and helped me remember the rules well enough for me to play again.
We’re so used to asking models about anything that we take their vast knowledge for granted.</p><p>How do models remember so much information? And <em>how much can they store?</em></p><p>In this post, I’ll go through an intuitive way to look at linear maps, MLPs, and self-attention as different kinds of memory stores. From this intuition we can derive some heuristics for how much memory each layer can store.</p><hr><p>The key property that characterizes a “good” memory store is its ability to recall what it stored. After all, even if we could store infinitely many facts, it’s not very helpful if we can’t recall them when we need to.
The more memories we store, the more they <em>interfere</em> with each other when we try to recall one. So throughout the post we’ll focus on one quantity, the interference-to-signal ratio, which measures how much the other memories get in the way during recall. The number of memories a store can hold before the interference grows as large as the signal is its <strong>capacity</strong>.</p><p>We’ll develop this perspective and show how you can go from a linear layer to something better, an MLP, which improves both capacity and recall, and then all the way to self-attention, which has very good recall and can store a potentially unbounded number of memories. With 64-dimensional keys, that’s a jump from about 64 memories, to about 2,700, to over a million.</p><p>Here are the terms we’ll use throughout. A <strong>memory store</strong> is a function $f$ that holds $M$ memories, for $i = 1, \ldots, M$. A <strong>memory</strong> is one key-value pair: a <strong>key</strong> $k_i \in \reals^{d_k}$, used for lookup based on similarity, and a <strong>value</strong> $v_i \in \reals^{d_v}$, which is what we want to retrieve. To use $f$, we <strong>query</strong> it with an input $x$, and it <strong>recalls</strong> an output $f(x)$. A good memory store recalls $v_i$ when we query it with $k_i$, or with another vector similar to $k_i$. In the literature, a memory store that recalls by similarity is called an <em>associative memory</em>.</p><h2 id="an-outer-product-memory-store">An outer product memory store<a href="#an-outer-product-memory-store" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>The most basic memory store is linear: it simply accumulates memories via outer products,</p><p>$$W = \sum_{i=1}^{M} v_i k_i\transpose = V\transpose K$$</p><p>where the rows of $K \in \reals^{M\times d_k}$ and $V \in \reals^{M\times d_v}$ are the keys $k_i\transpose$ and the values $v_i\transpose$.<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup></p><p>To store a new memory $(k, v)$, we simply add $vk\transpose$ to the existing weights.<sup id="fnref:2"><a href="#fn:2" role="doc-noteref">2</a></sup></p><details><summary>Aside: a gradient descent step writes a memory</summary><p>Take a linear layer $y = Wx$ inside a network with a scalar loss $L$, and let $\bar y = \grad L(y)$ be the gradient of the loss with respect to the layer’s output. The gradient with respect to $W$ is $\bar y x\transpose$, so a step with learning rate $\eta$ is</p>$$W \leftarrow W - \eta\, \bar y x\transpose.$$<p>This recurrence is a memory update: it updates the weights with a new memory with key $x$ and value $-\eta\bar y$.
For more on this view, see <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.05798">Irie, Csordás &amp; Schmidhuber</a>, who express a linear layer trained by gradient descent as attention over all of its training inputs.</p></details><p>To query the memory store with an input $x$, we multiply the input by the matrix:</p><p>$$f(x) = V\transpose K x = \sum_{i=1}^{M} v_i\,(k_i\transpose x).$$</p><p>So recall is a lookup by similarity to the stored keys: we compare the query with every key, and return the sum of the values weighted by those similarities.</p><h3 id="recall-picks-up-interference">Recall picks up interference<a href="#recall-picks-up-interference" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3><p>So storing memories is easy: just keep adding outer products to the weights. But what happens when we try to recall one?</p><p>Let’s assume for simplicity that every key is normalized to have length 1, i.e. $k_j\transpose k_j = 1$. Then</p><p>$$f(k_j) = \underbrace{v_j}_{\text{signal}} + \underbrace{\sum_{i\neq j} v_i\,(k_i\transpose k_j)}_{\text{interference}},$$</p><p>meaning we get back the value of the <strong>target memory</strong>, the one whose key we queried with, plus interference from every other memory whose key is similar to the query.</p><p>If the keys were all orthonormal, the interference would be zero and recall would be exact. But $\reals^{d_k}$ only has $d_k$ orthogonal directions, so we cannot get perfect recall once we store more memories than the key dimension: $M &gt; d_k$.</p><p>What is the outer-product store’s capacity? Here’s an intuitive argument. There are $M-1$ interference terms. If the keys are random unit vectors, each similarity $k_i\transpose k_j$ is about $\pm 1/\sqrt{d_k}$. If the values are random too, with similar lengths, the $M-1$ interference terms add up like a random walk, so the interference’s squared length is about $(M-1)/d_k$ times the signal’s, and</p><p>$$\frac{\norm{\text{interference}}}{\norm{\text{signal}}} \sim \sqrt{\frac{M}{d_k}}.$$</p><p>Setting this ratio to 1 gives a capacity of</p><p>$$M \approx d_k,$$</p><p>once we store as many memories as the key dimension, the interference is as large as the signal, drowning it out.</p><p>Each bar below is one stored memory’s weight in a single recall, relative to the target memory’s weight (in red). Every bar adds a bit of its value to the recall. Drag $M$ past $d_k = 64$ to watch the small bars pile up until recall fails. The other buttons switch to memory stores we’ll build later in the post.</p><figure id="interactive" role="group" aria-label="Interactive figure: the weight each stored memory gets in one recall, and how much of the recalled vector is interference"><div><p><label>stored memories <i>M</i> = <span>32</span>
</label>
<label>sharpness <i>β</i> = <span>8</span>
</label></p></div><p><span><span></span>the target memory</span><span>each other stored memory, weight relative to the target memory</span></p><p><span>interference / signal = <span></span></span>
<span><span></span><span title="interference = signal"></span></span>
<span>recall fails</span></p></figure><h2 id="how-do-we-reduce-interference">How do we reduce interference?<a href="#how-do-we-reduce-interference" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>Even below its capacity of about $d_k$ memories, the outer-product store produces significant interference during recall.<sup id="fnref:3"><a href="#fn:3" role="doc-noteref">3</a></sup> To raise the capacity, or recall more cleanly below it, we need to <em>reduce interference</em>.</p><p>The brute-force way is to make the key dimension $d_k$ larger, since a linear store’s capacity grows with $d_k$. How large $d_k$ can be is governed by hardware constraints.</p><p>Alternatively, we can make algorithmic improvements. We can store memories more carefully: the outer-product store adds each memory without looking at the others, so it does nothing to cancel their interference. Or we can recall more selectively: every memory in a linear store contributes interference, even when its key has low similarity to the query, so we can improve recall by suppressing the less similar keys.</p><p>In the rest of the post, we’ll see that storing outer product memories more carefully gives us the optimal linear memory store, which has about twice the capacity. Recalling selectively via nonlinearities gives us MLPs and self-attention.</p><h2 id="the-optimal-linear-memory-store">The optimal linear memory store<a href="#the-optimal-linear-memory-store" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>The outer-product store writes each memory without regard to the others. Instead, we can choose the weights $W$ so that recall fits all of the stored memories at once. This is a linear regression problem:<sup id="fnref:4"><a href="#fn:4" role="doc-noteref">4</a></sup></p><p>$$\operatorname*{minimize}_W \sum_{i=1}^M \norm{W k_i - v_i}_2^2.$$</p><p>When we store more memories than the key dimension, $M &gt; d_k$, the least-squares solution is</p><p>$$W = \parens*{\sum_i v_i k_i\transpose}\parens*{\sum_i k_i k_i\transpose}\inverse = V\transpose K\,(K\transpose K)\inverse.$$</p><p>This is the outer-product store $V\transpose K$ with one change: before we compare the query with the keys, we multiply it by $(K\transpose K)\inverse$, which undoes the keys’ covariance $K\transpose K$. This <em>whitens</em> the keys, so the store no longer overweights directions that many keys share.</p><p>When $M \leq d_k$, the regression can fit every memory exactly, as long as the keys are linearly independent. So the optimal store recalls with no interference at all, even when the keys aren’t orthogonal.</p><p>When $M &gt; d_k$, we start to get interference, since there are only $d_k$ directions for the memory store to work with. For random keys and values, our interference-to-signal ratio becomes</p><p>$$\frac{\norm{\text{interference}}}{\norm{\text{signal}}} \approx \sqrt{\frac{M}{d_k} - 1}.$$</p><p>This ratio equals 1 at</p><p>$$M = 2d_k,$$</p><p>so the optimal store’s capacity is <em>twice</em> as large as before!</p><details><summary>Aside: deriving the optimal linear memory store</summary><p><strong>When $M \leq d_k$.</strong> If the keys are linearly independent, the $M\times M$ matrix of inner products between keys, $KK\transpose$, is invertible, and</p>$$W = V\transpose (KK\transpose)\inverse K$$<p>fits every memory exactly. When $M &lt; d_k$, the problem is underconstrained and has infinitely many solutions, and this one is the min-norm solution. The rows $\tilde k_i\transpose$ of $(KK\transpose)\inverse K$ are known as the dual basis for the rows of $K$: by construction, $(KK\transpose)\inverse KK\transpose = I$, so $\tilde k_i\transpose k_j = \delta_{ij}$. Then $f(k_j) = \sum_i v_i\, \tilde k_i\transpose k_j = v_j$, so there is <em>no interference</em> and we have perfect recall.</p><p><strong>When $M &gt; d_k$.</strong> Setting the gradient of the objective to zero gives $W K\transpose K = V\transpose K$, so $W = V\transpose K\,(K\transpose K)\inverse$.
Both solutions are $W = V\transpose (K\transpose)\pinv$, where $A\pinv$ denotes the pseudoinverse of $A$: $(K\transpose)\pinv = (KK\transpose)\inverse K$ when the keys are linearly independent, and $(K\transpose)\pinv = K(K\transpose K)\inverse$ when they span $\reals^{d_k}$.</p><p>Now we can analyze the interference of $f(k_j) = V\transpose K\,(K\transpose K)\inverse k_j$.
For a stored key $k_j$, the weight on each value $v_i$ is $k_i\transpose (K\transpose K)\inverse k_j$, which is exactly entry $(i, j)$ of the $M\times M$ matrix $P = K(K\transpose K)\inverse K\transpose$:</p>$$f(k_j) = \sum_{i=1}^M v_i\, P_{ij} = \underbrace{P_{jj}\, v_j}_{\text{signal}} + \underbrace{\sum_{i\neq j} P_{ij}\, v_i}_{\text{interference}}.$$<p>$P$ is the projection onto the column space of $K$, a $d_k$-dimensional subspace of $\reals^M$. Two facts about projections help us analyze the interference-to-signal ratio:</p><ul><li><strong>The signal term:</strong> By the cyclic property of the trace,
$$\sum_{j=1}^M P_{jj} = \trace{K(K\transpose K)\inverse K\transpose} = \trace{(K\transpose K)\inverse K\transpose K} = \trace{I_{d_k}} = d_k,$$
so the $M$ signal weights add up to $d_k$. If the keys are random, no key is special, so $P_{jj} \approx d_k/M$.</li><li><strong>The interference term:</strong> $P$ is a symmetric projection matrix, so $P\transpose P = P^2 = P$. Then
$$\sum_{i\neq j} P_{ij}^2 = \sum_{i=1}^M P_{ij}^2 - P_{jj}^2 = (P\transpose P)_{jj} - P_{jj}^2 = P_{jj} - P_{jj}^2 \approx \frac{d_k}{M} - \parens*{\frac{d_k}{M}}^2.$$</li></ul><p>If the values are random with similar lengths,</p>$$\frac{\norm{\text{interference}}}{\norm{\text{signal}}} \approx \frac{\sqrt{P_{jj} - P_{jj}^2}}{P_{jj}} = \sqrt{\frac{M}{d_k} - 1}.$$<p>Compared to the outer-product store’s $\sqrt{M/d_k}$, the $-1$ is the $d_k$ memories’ worth of interference that the optimal store cancels.</p></details><p>We can check our predictions numerically. We randomly sample $M$ memories with unit-length keys in $d_k = 64$ dimensions, query the memory store with one of the stored keys, and measure the interference-to-signal ratio:</p><figure><svg viewBox="0 0 640 340" role="img" aria-label="Interference-to-signal ratio against M for two linear memories with d = 64. With the query equal to a stored key, the outer-product memory's interference reaches the signal at M = 64, and the optimal least-squares memory is exact up to M = 64 and reaches the signal near M = 128."><rect x="52" y="14" width="438" height="153.18367346938774"></rect><text x="60" y="31">recall fails: more interference than signal</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="280.6530612244897" y2="280.6530612244897"></line><text x="44" y="284.6530612244897" text-anchor="end">0.1</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="167.18367346938774" y2="167.18367346938774"></line><text x="44" y="171.18367346938774" text-anchor="end">1</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="53.71428571428572" y2="53.71428571428572"></line><text x="44" y="57.71428571428572" text-anchor="end">10</text><line x1="52" x2="52" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="52" y="311" text-anchor="middle">16</text><line x1="139.60000000000002" x2="139.60000000000002" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="139.60000000000002" y="311" text-anchor="middle">64</text><line x1="227.20000000000002" x2="227.20000000000002" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="227.20000000000002" y="311" 