-s="1" data-i="1" cx="95.80000000000001" cy="292" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 32: interference/signal = 0.00</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="2" cx="139.60000000000002" cy="292" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 64: interference/signal = 0.00</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="3" cx="140.5797102106463" cy="289.7859660327351" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 65: interference/signal = 0.08</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="4" cx="141.54446242790027" cy="263.37959015254273" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 66: interference/signal = 0.14</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="5" cx="143.43087244676488" cy="240.67820612756896" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 68: interference/signal = 0.23</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="6" cx="147.04271506317326" cy="219.29295202811693" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 72: interference/signal = 0.35</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="7" cx="153.70045055606644" cy="202.53727373640476" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 80: interference/signal = 0.49</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="8" cx="165.22135753158665" cy="184.362262181942" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 96: interference/signal = 0.71</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="9" cx="174.96214558612303" cy="174.62302805044064" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 112: interference/signal = 0.86</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="10" cx="183.4" cy="167.4094447621928" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 128: interference/signal = 1.00</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="11" cx="227.20000000000002" cy="140.40769059192934" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 256: interference/signal = 1.72</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="12" cx="271" cy="119.49424120773124" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 512: interference/signal = 2.63</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="13" cx="314.8" cy="101.30063015156698" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 1024: interference/signal = 3.81</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="14" cx="358.59999999999997" cy="82.65905597434251" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 2048: interference/signal = 5.56</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="15" cx="402.40000000000003" cy="65.03490442955558" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 4096: interference/signal = 7.95</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="16" cx="446.2" cy="47.538156555337714" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 8192: interference/signal = 11.34</title></circle><circle data-s="1" data-i="17" cx="490" cy="30.82070770996541" r="3.5"><title>optimal (least-squares) memory, M = 16384: interference/signal = 15.91</title></circle></g><text data-s="1" data-at="-1" data-dy="20" x="498" y="50.82070770996541">optimal (least squares)</text></svg></figure><p>The outer-product store reaches its capacity at $M \approx d_k = 64$. The optimal store recalls exactly up to $M = d_k$. Past that, its interference rises from zero like $\sqrt{M/d_k - 1}$, and the store reaches its capacity near $M = 2d_k = 128$, as predicted. Since it cancels a fixed number of memories’ worth of interference, the gap between the two stores shrinks as $M$ grows.</p><p>This optimal memory store is still linear in the similarities though. It must scale small and large similarities by the same factor. To do better, we need to change how the store recalls.</p><h2 id="mlp-as-a-nonlinear-memory-store">MLP as a nonlinear memory store<a href="#mlp-as-a-nonlinear-memory-store" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>A linear memory store recalls</p><p>$$f(x) = \sum_{i=1}^M v_i (k_i\transpose x).$$</p><p>If we had a way to suppress the weight when $k_i\transpose x$ is small, then we can reduce the amount of interference from low similarity keys.</p><p>Let’s pass each similarity through a nonlinear function $\sigma$ before computing the weighted sum of values:</p><p>$$f(x) = \sum_{i=1}^{M} v_i\,\sigma(k_i\transpose x) = V\transpose \sigma(Kx).$$</p><p>We just rediscovered a two-layer MLP from first principles! The rows of the first weight matrix are the keys, the columns of the second weight matrix are the values. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.01470">Sukhbaatar et al.</a> pointed out this resemblance between feedforward layers and memory stores, and <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.14913">Geva et al.</a> found that the feedforward layers in trained transformers often work this way, with keys firing on recognizable patterns in the input.</p><p>Ideally, the gating function $\sigma$ is chosen such that when the similarity is low ($k_i\transpose x \sim 0$ or even when $k_i\transpose x &lt; 0$), its corresponding term in the sum gets suppressed to reduce interference. A ReLU activation zeros out keys with negative similarities, and adding a bias allows shifting that threshold<sup id="fnref:5"><a href="#fn:5" role="doc-noteref">5</a></sup>. A squared ReLU activation further suppresses the keys with similarities close to zero. A similarity of 0.1 becomes 0.01, while a similarity of 1 stays 1. In fact, squared ReLUs are one of the lesser known but established-at-scale improvements to MLP activations (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.08668">So et al.</a>).</p><p>A squared ReLU MLP is a GLU MLP whose gate and input projection share weights, since $\operatorname{ReLU}(Kx)^2 = \operatorname{ReLU}(Kx) \odot Kx$. The SwiGLU MLPs in today’s language models give the gate its own weights and use a Swish activation in place of the ReLU.</p><p>In practice, MLPs store facts in superposition: many facts share the same weights, and the output is a blend of many memories. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.10034">Garcia et al.</a> build one explicitly: the hidden units of a GLU MLP compute random features $\phi(x)$ of the input, the second weight matrix is an outer-product store $\sum_i v_i \phi(k_i)\transpose$ over those features, and the MLP stores far more facts than it has hidden units. Still, trained transformers’ ReLU MLPs can activate as few as 1% of their hidden units for a given input, so each recall draws on a small fraction of the memories (<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.06313">Li et al.</a>).</p><details><summary>The transformer authors already knew this</summary><p>The original transformer paper made this connection too, but only in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762v2">v2</a> and <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762v3">v3</a> on arXiv. There, Vaswani et al. describe the feedforward layer as “a kind of attention,” with the rows of its two weight matrices as the keys and values, and ReLU in place of softmax. They even tried replacing the feedforward layers with attention over learned key-value parameters, and got about the same translation quality: one version did slightly worse, another slightly better, though the first one trained about 30% slower. The passage is gone from every later version of the paper. We’ll get to how self-attention relates to all this below.</p></details><h3 id="reducing-interference-with-higher-powers">Reducing interference with higher powers<a href="#reducing-interference-with-higher-powers" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3><p>Squaring the similarities works because it shrinks low similarity weights much more than large ones. We can push this further with $\sigma(s) = \operatorname{ReLU}(s)^p$. The signal stays the same, since $1^p = 1$, while an interference term with similarity $1/\sqrt{d_k}$ shrinks to $d_k^{-p/2}$. The $M$ interference terms add up like a random walk, so the interference-to-signal ratio is</p><p>$$\frac{\norm{\text{interference}}}{\norm{\text{signal}}} \approx \sqrt{\frac{M}{d_k^p}}.$$</p><p>This means the capacity is about</p><p>$$M \approx d_k^p$$</p><p>memories, <em>geometrically</em> more than before!</p><p>To check this, we run the same experiment as before for five choices of $\sigma$:</p><figure><svg viewBox="0 0 640 340" role="img" aria-label="Interference-to-signal ratio against the number of stored memories M, on log-log axes. With the query equal to a stored key, the linear memory's interference reaches the signal at about M = 64, the ReLU memory's at about 128, the squared-ReLU memory's and the SwiGLU memory's at about 3,000, and the cubed-ReLU memory's beyond 16,384."><rect x="52" y="14" width="438" height="109.51515151515153"></rect><text x="60" y="31">recall fails: more interference than signal</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="292" y2="292"></line><text x="44" y="296" text-anchor="end">0.01</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="207.75757575757575" y2="207.75757575757575"></line><text x="44" y="211.75757575757575" text-anchor="end">0.1</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="123.51515151515153" y2="123.51515151515153"></line><text x="44" y="127.51515151515153" text-anchor="end">1</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="39.27272727272728" y2="39.27272727272728"></line><text x="44" y="43.27272727272728" text-anchor="end">10</text><line x1="52" x2="52" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="52" y="311" text-anchor="middle">16</text><line x1="139.60000000000002" x2="139.60000000000002" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="139.60000000000002" y="311" text-anchor="middle">64</text><line x1="227.20000000000002" x2="227.20000000000002" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="227.20000000000002" y="311" text-anchor="middle">256</text><line x1="314.8" x2="314.8" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="314.8" y="311" text-anchor="middle">1024</text><line x1="402.40000000000003" x2="402.40000000000003" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="402.40000000000003" y="311" text-anchor="middle">4096</text><line x1="490" x2="490" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="490" y="311" text-anchor="middle">16384</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="292" y2="292"></line><text x="271" y="334" text-anchor="middle">number