= 128: interference/signal = 0.05</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="4" cx="227.20000000000002" cy="217.44790952143418" r="3.5"><title>ReLU(s)³, M = 256: interference/signal = 0.08</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="5" cx="271" cy="203.4815496320477" r="3.5"><title>ReLU(s)³, M = 512: interference/signal = 0.11</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="6" cx="314.8" cy="190.41072350009006" r="3.5"><title>ReLU(s)³, M = 1024: interference/signal = 0.16</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="7" cx="358.59999999999997" cy="177.3511381846475" r="3.5"><title>ReLU(s)³, M = 2048: interference/signal = 0.23</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="8" cx="402.40000000000003" cy="164.61749838709252" r="3.5"><title>ReLU(s)³, M = 4096: interference/signal = 0.33</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="9" cx="446.2" cy="152.08514508837914" r="3.5"><title>ReLU(s)³, M = 8192: interference/signal = 0.46</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="10" cx="490" cy="139.20712293185568" r="3.5"><title>ReLU(s)³, M = 16384: interference/signal = 0.65</title></circle></g><text data-s="4" data-at="-1" data-dy="4" x="500" y="143.20712293185568">ReLU(<tspan>s</tspan>)<tspan dy="-6">3</tspan></text></svg></figure><p>Since the interference grows like $\sqrt{M}$, the lines are nearly parallel on a log-log plot. Only their height changes, and with it the capacity, where each line crosses 1:</p><ul><li>Linear: the capacity is $M \approx d_k = 64$.</li><li>ReLU: zeroing the negative similarities shrinks the interference by $\sqrt 2$, so the capacity grows to $M \approx 2d_k = 128$.</li><li>Squared ReLU: each interference term shrinks from about $1/\sqrt{d_k}$ to about $1/d_k$, so the capacity grows to $M \approx 2d_k^2/3 \approx 2700$, about 40 times the linear store’s.</li><li>SwiGLU MLP, with the gate sharing the input projection’s weights as in the squared ReLU, $\sigma(s) = \operatorname{Swish}(s)\cdot s = s^2/(1 + e^{-s})$: its capacity nearly matches the squared ReLU’s.</li><li>Cubed ReLU: each interference term shrinks to about $d_k^{-3/2}$, so the capacity grows to $M \approx 2d_k^3/15 \approx 35000$.</li></ul><h2 id="self-attention-as-a-non-parametric-memory-store">Self-attention as a non-parametric memory store<a href="#self-attention-as-a-non-parametric-memory-store" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>Raising the weights to a power increases the capacity to about $d_k^p$. But what if we could push this to the limit? Rather than pick one power, we can use infinitely many of them. Indeed, the exponential is a weighted sum of every power,</p><p>$$e^{\beta s} = 1 + \beta s + \frac{(\beta s)^2}{2} + \frac{(\beta s)^3}{6} + \cdots = \sum_{p=0}^{\infty} \frac{(\beta s)^p}{p!},$$</p><p>with the sharpness $\beta$ setting the weight on the higher powers. Here is the same experiment with $\beta = \sqrt{d_k}$, the value self-attention uses,<sup id="fnref:6"><a href="#fn:6" role="doc-noteref">6</a></sup> for the exponential and its series truncated after 2, 3 and 4 terms:</p><figure><svg viewBox="0 0 640 340" role="img" aria-label="Interference-to-signal ratio against M for the exponential score with sharpness the square root of d, and its series truncated after 2, 3 and 4 terms, with the linear memory for reference, and a slider for the query's similarity to its stored key."><rect x="52" y="14" width="438" height="97.3"></rect><text x="60" y="31">recall fails: more interference than signal</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="250.29999999999998" y2="250.29999999999998"></line><text x="44" y="254.29999999999998" text-anchor="end">0.01</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="180.79999999999995" y2="180.79999999999995"></line><text x="44" y="184.79999999999995" text-anchor="end">0.1</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="111.3" y2="111.3"></line><text x="44" y="115.3" text-anchor="end">1</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="41.8" y2="41.8"></line><text x="44" y="45.8" text-anchor="end">10</text><line x1="52" x2="52" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x