title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="1" cx="95.80000000000001" cy="276.96955433292123" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 32: interference/signal = 0.00</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="2" cx="139.60000000000002" cy="263.47949671912704" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 64: interference/signal = 0.01</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="3" cx="183.4" cy="252.42559409139827" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 128: interference/signal = 0.01</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="4" cx="227.20000000000002" cy="241.77867286146866" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 256: interference/signal = 0.01</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="5" cx="271" cy="229.92666690016028" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 512: interference/signal = 0.02</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="6" cx="314.8" cy="219.0076869718044" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 1024: interference/signal = 0.03</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="7" cx="358.59999999999997" cy="207.8798399199725" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 2048: interference/signal = 0.04</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="8" cx="402.40000000000003" cy="197.72436447123863" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 4096: interference/signal = 0.06</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="9" cx="446.2" cy="187.76855800485458" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 8192: interference/signal = 0.08</title></circle><circle data-s="4" data-i="10" cx="490" cy="177.25026611653544" r="3.5"><title>exp(βs) (self-attention), M = 16384: interference/signal = 0.11</title></circle></g><text data-s="4" data-at="-1" data-dy="4" x="500" y="181.25026611653544">e<tspan dy="-6">βs</tspan><tspan dy="6">&nbsp;(self-attention)</tspan></text></svg></figure><p>With 2 terms, the capacity is about 40, below the linear store’s, since the constant term gives every key a weight of about 1. With 3 terms it’s about 450, and with 4 terms about 3,100, close to the squared ReLU’s. The full exponential stays far below the dashed line, so its capacity is past the edge of the chart.</p><p>To find the exponential’s capacity, we query with a stored key $k_j$, so the target memory has similarity 1 and weight $w_j = e^{\beta}$. Every other memory has weight $w_i = e^{\beta s_i}$, where for random unit keys the similarity $s_i$ is roughly Gaussian with mean 0 and variance $1/d_k$. With random values of similar lengths,</p><p>$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\norm{\text{interference}}}{\norm{\text{signal}}}
&amp;\approx \sqrt{\sum_{i\neq j} \parens*{\frac{w_i}{w_j}}^2} &amp;&amp; \text{interference terms add up like a random walk} \\
&amp;= \sqrt{\sum_{i\neq j} e^{2\beta (s_i - 1)}} &amp;&amp; w_i / w_j = e^{\beta s_i} / e^{\beta} \\
&amp;\approx \sqrt{M\, e^{-2\beta}\, \ev{e^{2\beta s}}} &amp;&amp; \text{average over the } M \text{ other memories} \\
&amp;= \sqrt{M\, e^{-2\beta}\, e^{2\beta^2/d_k}} &amp;&amp; \ev{e^{ts}} = e^{t^2/(2d_k)} \text{ for } s \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1/d_k) \\
&amp;= \sqrt{M}\, e^{\beta^2/d_k - \beta}.
\end{aligned}
$$</p><p>Setting the ratio to 1 gives a capacity of</p><p>$$M \approx e^{2\beta(1 - \beta/d_k)},$$</p><p>growing exponentially with $\beta$ as long as $\beta$ is well below $d_k$, rather than polynomially in $d_k$ like before! With $\beta = \sqrt{d_k} = 8$, the capacity is about $e^{14} \approx 1.2$ million memories, nearly 20,000 times the linear store’s. A longer run of the same experiment puts the capacity at about 1.2 million too.</p><p>In practice, we may not want to naively exponentiate numbers in our implementation, since small numbers can underflow and large numbers can overflow. Fortunately, the interference only depends on the <em>ratios</em> between weights, as the chain above shows, so we can divide every weight by their sum without changing the interference-to-signal ratio, while ensuring numerical stability:</p><p>$$f(x) = \sum_{i=1}^{M} v_i\,\frac{\exp(\beta\,k_i\transpose x)}{\sum_j \exp(\beta\,k_j\transpose x)} = V\transpose \operatorname{softmax}(\beta K x).$$</p><p><em>This is self-attention</em>.<sup id="fnref:7"><a href="#fn:7" role="doc-noteref">7</a></sup></p><p>Unlike an MLP, whose keys and values are trained weights, self-attention computes its keys and values from the tokens in its context. Each token stores one memory, so the number of memories $M$ is the context length. That’s what makes self-attention non-parametric: its memories aren’t parameters, and the store grows with the context.</p><p>Here’s the bar figure again, starting with softmax. At $\beta = 8$, recall never fails within the slider’s range, so lower $\beta$ to 2 and raise $M$: recall fails after a few dozen memories. Each doubling of $M$ needs a bit more $\beta$ to keep recall working, which is the idea behind the next section.</p><figure role="group" aria-label="Interactive figure: the weight each stored memory gets in one recall, and how much of the recalled vector is interference"><div><p><label>stored memories <i>M</i> = <span>32</span>
</label>
<label>sharpness <i>β</i> = <span>8</span>
</label></p></div><p><span><span></span>the target memory</span><span>each other stored memory, weight relative to the target memory</span></p><p><span>interference / signal = <span></span></span>
<span><span></span><span title="interference = signal"></span></span>
<span>recall fails</span></p></figure><h3 id="handling-many-key-value-pairs-in-long-context">Handling many key-value pairs in long context<a href="#handling-many-key-value-pairs-in-long-context" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h3><p>Since $\beta$ determines how hard we suppress less similar keys, we can increase $\beta$ as the number of key-value pairs increases. Why not just make $\beta$ huge? As $\beta \to \infty$, softmax becomes a hard argmax that picks only the most similar key, which isn’t differentiable, and a very large $\beta$ already makes training work poorly, since the softmax saturates and the gradients to every other key vanish. So we raise $\beta$ only as much as $M$ requires. Scaling the attention logits with $\log M$ is a trick for long contexts that does this. There are a few versions of it, and each sets $\beta$ relative to a fixed reference length $L$, usually the training length, so that $\beta$ keeps its trained value when $M = L$:</p><ul><li><a href="https://kexue.fm/archives/8823">Su (2021)</a> derives a scale of $\log M$ from keeping the attention’s entropy constant as $M$ grows, normalized to match standard attention at $M = L$. The logits are multiplied by $\log M / \log L$ at every length. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.19399">Scalable-Softmax</a> has the same form, multiplying the logits by $s \log M$ with $s$ learned.</li><li>The original <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16609">Qwen</a> models use LogN-Scaling, which applies the same factor only once the context is longer than $L$.</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.15383">Qwen2.5-1M</a> uses YaRN’s attention scaling, which multiplies the logits by $(0.1 \log(M/L) + 1)^2$, so it grows like $(\log M)^2$.</li></ul><p>Here’s how each rule does in our experiment, with $\beta = \sqrt{d_k} = 8$ at $L = 1024$ memories:</p><figure><svg viewBox="0 0 640 340" role="img" aria-label="Interference-to-signal ratio of softmax attention against M up to about 2 million pairs, for d = 64, under four rules for the sharpness. With the query equal to the stored key and a fixed sharpness of 8, the interference reaches the signal near 1.2 million pairs; the log-scaled rules fall as M grows."><rect x="52" y="14" width="438" height="32.705882352941174"></rect><text x="60" y="31">recall fails: more interference than signal</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="264.74509803921563" y2="264.74509803921563"></line><text x="44" y="268.74509803921563" text-anchor="end">0.0001</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="210.23529411764704" y2="210.23529411764704"></line><text x="44" y="214.23529411764704" text-anchor="end">0.001</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="155.72549019607845" y2="155.72549019607845"></line><text x="44" y="159.72549019607845" text-anchor="end">0.01</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="101.21568627450979" y2="101.21568627450979"></line><text x="44" y="105.21568627450979" text-anchor="end">0.1</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="46.705882352941174" y2="46.705882352941174"></line><text x="44" y="50.705882352941174" text-anchor="end">1</text><line x1="52" x2="52" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="52" y="311" text-anchor="middle">16</text><line x1="155.05882352941177" x2="155.05882352941177" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="155.05882352941177" y="311" text-anchor="middle">256</text><line x1="258.11764705882354" x2="258.11764705882354" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="258.11764705882354" y="311" text-anchor="middle">4K</text><line x1="361.1764705882353" x2="361.1764705882353" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="361.1764705882353" y="311" text-anchor="middle">64K</text><line x1="464.2352941176471" x2="464.2352941176471" y1="292" y2="296"></line><text x="464.2352941176471" y="311" text-anchor="middle">1M</text><line x1="52" x2="490" y1="292" y2="292"></line><text x="271" y="334" text-anchor="middle">number of stored memories&nbsp;<tspan>M</tspan>&nbsp;(key dimension&nbsp;<tspan>d</tspan><tspan dy="4">k</tspan><tspan dy="-4">&nbsp;= 64)</tspan></text><text transfor